From 21dd71c710fe9600979330820603abffcfba6de1 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: z060142 <z060142@yahoo.com.tw>
Date: Sat, 29 Aug 2026 02:26:57 +0800
Subject: [PATCH 099/119] fix: the sky's Mie peak wrote Inf into an fp16
 target, and the bloom's firefly knee turned the sun into a flat bun

TEST-CHECKLIST F2/F5: with the sun in frame, one level shows a flat white
"bun"-shaped halo and the airfield shows sun shafts over the whole screen.
Three faults, one origin.

1. THE WRITER. The procedural sky has no sun sprite - what reads as the sun IS
the Mie phase function, pow(miePart_g2_1 + miePart_g_2 * cosine, -1.5), whose
base collapses towards (1-g)^2 as the view meets the sun and whose peak is
unbounded by construction. Stock CE never noticed: the tone map's exposure
divided it down first. Under the pre-exposure it is multiplied up, and at a
daylight EV the disc lands past fp16's 65504. Every writer that can do this is
now clamped at its output - the sky pass (which also answers NaN with black),
and the emissive term in Illum.cfx and MultiLayeredMaterials.cfx, where kcd/m2
times a night pre-exposure is the same story. New cvar
r_SceneReferredWriterClamp (default 2^15 = 32768, 0 = off; 2^16 is not
representable in fp16 and would still store Inf), published in
CV_SceneLuminanceWeightsDelta.w as SCENE_WRITER_CLAMP, 0 off the switch so
every branch folds away. ExecuteSceneReferredConversion enforces the same
ceiling on the whole target - NaN to 0, Inf and anything over to the ceiling -
because it is the last pass before anything AVERAGES the buffer, and the writer
list can never be proved complete. It sits in the else of r_SceneReferredDebug
2 so that instrument still shows what arrives broken.

2. THE BUN is a per-pass clamp, and it is the bloom downsample's firefly
suppression: Karis's x/(1+L) with its asymptote at a literal 1.0, a number that
meant display white. Under the pre-exposure every tap over about ten compresses
into the same sliver below 1 and comes back at the same brightness, so the sun,
its halo and the sky around it are one flat plateau. The knee moves to the
camera's white (SCENE_METER_CEILING, 14 stops over grey; 1.0 and bit-identical
off the switch). Said out loud in the note: on the ReC path this stops being a
denoiser and becomes an overflow guard.

3. THE SHAFTS. The mask writes skyVisibility * dot(sceneCol, 1.32) and the
composite multiplies it by the sun's exposed radiance - so the mask has to be
dimensionless, and on the stock path it was, by accident, because the UNORM
working set saturated it at 1. S2 made that set fp16 and the saturation went
with it. The mask is bounded at mid grey again (the one level the pre-exposure
pins), the pass now binds the per-view constant buffer, and the composited
shaft radiance is capped at the camera's white - a bound on an approximation,
labelled as one: shafts are atmospheric and the sensor clip is their real
limit, but their amount slider is a display fraction with no calibration behind
it.

decisions/s8-fp16-writers.md and the round-3 addendum to
decisions/s2-sun-shafts.md carry the approaches not taken.

Co-Authored-By: Claude Opus <noreply@anthropic.com>
