+	// Scene-referred writer clamp (decisions/s8-fp16-writers.md). THE sky writer that can leave
+	// an Inf in the fp16 HDR target, and the origin of both round-2 reports: the flat white
+	// "bun" around the sun and the full-screen sun shafts.
+	//
+	// The day-sky gradient has no sun sprite. What you see as the sun IS the Mie phase function,
+	//   miePhase = (1 + cos^2) * pow(miePart_g2_1 + miePart_g_2 * cosine, -1.5)
+	// whose base goes to pow(miePart, -2/3) * (1 - g)^2 as the view direction meets the sun
+	// direction, and with the Mie asymmetry g close to 1 that is a very small number raised to
+	// -1.5. The peak is unbounded by construction - a real forward-scattering lobe is - and
+	// stock CE never noticed, because the tone map's exposure divided it down before anything
+	// looked at it. Under the pre-exposure it is multiplied UP instead, and at a daylight EV the
+	// disc lands far past 65504.
+	//
+	// A min() rather than a saturate: everything below the ceiling is untouched, so the sun is
+	// still a very bright disc with a soft falloff, which is what it should be. NaN is caught
+	// separately because min(NaN, c) is not specified to return c.
+	if (SCENE_WRITER_CLAMP > 0)
+	{
+		// min() already answers +Inf with the ceiling (IEEE: min(inf, c) = c), which is the right
+		// answer for a scattering peak that ran away. NaN is a different failure and reads black.
+		const half3 cSky = OUT.Color.rgb;
+		OUT.Color.rgb = any(isnan(cSky)) ? (half3)0 : min(cSky, (half3)SCENE_WRITER_CLAMP);
+	}
+
 	return OUT;
 }
 
